## Section: The -ν Rule

<section id="movable-nu">
<purpose>Fine-tuning: when to keep or drop final ν on articles</purpose>

<heading>
The -ν Rule
<subtitle>Fine-tuning: when to keep or drop final ν on articles</subtitle>
</heading>

<alert variant="info">
The Rule: The final -ν on τον/την stays before vowels and certain consonants (κ, π, τ, γκ, μπ, ντ, τσ, τζ, ξ, ψ). Before other consonants, it can be dropped.
</alert>

<examples-grid>
<column title="Keep the -ν" colour="correct">
| Example | Reason |
|---------|--------|
| τον άντρα | Before vowel (α) |
| τον καφέ | Before κ |
| την πόλη | Before π |
| στον τοίχο | Before τ |
</column>

<column title="Drop the -ν" colour="incorrect">
| Example | Reason |
|---------|--------|
| τη μητέρα | Before μ |
| το σπίτι | Before σ |
| τη φίλη | Before φ |
| τη χώρα | Before χ |
</column>
</examples-grid>

<note>
**Practical advice:** When in doubt, keep the -ν. You'll never be wrong, and it's clearer in writing.
</note>
</section>

---

## Content Guidelines

1. **Article lookup first** - Most common need is "what's the accusative of ο?"
2. **Gender hints** - Help learners predict gender from noun endings
3. **Carousel for paradigms** - Don't overwhelm with all patterns at once
4. **Movable nu is optional** - Mark as "fine-tuning" for advanced learners
5. **Connect to practice** - CTA links to /practice/speed?focus=articles
